#edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100185 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100185 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100185 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186510834 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100185 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "8efadd76-a965-516a-bd05-82f7d6c9e07e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100545 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186100546 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100545 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186100547 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100545 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186100548 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100545 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186100550 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100545 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186510979 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100545 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100545 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100545 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186510979 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100545 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "66e4fa29-0429-5e20-b824-7bf28e1113c8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100546 #edb/ref 1080 "$$850$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100546 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100546 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "bc079cff-d7da-5a87-b324-3586bd45da20" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100547 #edb/ref 1080 "$$640$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100547 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100547 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c0a0efd8-50ec-59dd-9e81-0c4bfa6eebc5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100548 #edb/ref 1080 "$$483.5$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100548 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100548 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d055eaa7-a9f5-5d9e-932a-3b0feca2a7ce" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100550 #edb/ref 1080 "$$219.7$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100550 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100550 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f8616558-a163-56ed-b4e0-530b461b2845" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100660 #edb/ref 1069 "e-357" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100660 #edb/ref 1071 "A company spends $C(x) = x^2 + 80x$ dollars to produce $x$ units of a certain product. The company sells the product for $\\[math] 180$ per unit. What is the maximum monthly profit that the factory can make given that it can produce at most $60$ units of product per month?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100660 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6c44b63d-72f8-599a-a89c-29fdc2227ab5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100660 #edb/ref 1074 "First, note that for selling $x$ units of the product per month, the company has a monthly revenue (income) of $R(x)=180\\cdot x=180 x$ dollars. \nTherefore, the profit function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\  & =180x-({x}^{2}+80x) \\\\  & =100x-{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the question statement, we know that the permitted domain is $0\\leq x \\leq 60.$ \n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $0 < x < 60.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\nP'(x)=100-2x,\n$$\n\nand solving $P'(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 100-2x & =0 \\\\ x & =50\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $x=50$ lies in the interval $0 <  x < 60.$ All that's left now is to confirm that $x=50$ does indeed give a maximum value of $P(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\nP''(x) = -2 <           0\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, the point $x=50$ is indeed a local maximum of $P(x).$ And since $P(x)$ is quadratic, we conclude that $x=50$ is a global maximum of $P(x).$ \n\nTherefore, the maximum monthly profit is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(50) & =100(50)-(50)^{2}=\\text{\\[}math]2\\,500.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100661 #edb/ref 1069 "e-359" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100661 #edb/ref 1071 "The director of a newspaper estimates that the company's profit, in hundreds of dollars, is given by \n\n$$\nP(x) = -x^2 + 44x + 5,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $x$ is the number of newspapers, in thousands, produced daily. Given that the company has the capacity to produce $30\\,000$ newspapers every day, how many newspapers per day should the company produce to maximize profit?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100661 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e47dca19-6772-532a-9955-ebe6f3db0d28" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100661 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we note that the permitted domain is \n\n$$\n0 \\leq x \\leq 30,\n$$\n\n since $x = 30$ corresponds to $30 \\cdot 1\\,000 = 30\\,000$ copies of the newspaper. \n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $0 <       x <       30.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\nP'(x) = -2x + 44,\n$$\n\nand solving $P'(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ -2x+44 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =44 \\\\ x & =22\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $x=22$ lies in the interval $0 \\leq x \\leq 30.$ All that's left now is to confirm that $x=22$ does indeed give a maximum value of $P(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\nP''(x) = -2 < 0.\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, the point $x=22$ is indeed a local maximum of $P(x).$ And since $P(x)$ is quadratic, we conclude that $x=22$ is a global maximum of $P(x).$ \n\nTherefore, the company should produce $22 \\cdot 1\\,000 = 22\\,000$ copies of the newspaper per day to maximize its profit." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100784 #edb/ref 1069 "e-7574" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100784 #edb/ref 1071 "A hotel has $21$ rooms. The manager has observed that when the room rate is $\\[math]12$ per night,  all rooms are occupied and, for every $\\[/math]1$ increase in the rate, a single room is vacated. If the maintenance of each rented room is $\\[math]3$ per night, what rate per room should the manager set to obtain the maximum profit, and what is the maximum profit?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100784 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a63d3bdb-ca20-51f5-b876-7ec8950a46dd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100784 #edb/ref 1074 "First, note that if there are $x$ *vacant* rooms, the hotel has $(21-x)$ occupied rooms, and the rate per room is $(12+x)$ dollars. So, the total revenue (in dollars) is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & ={{(21-x)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{occupied rooms}}\\times \\,\\,\\,{{(12+x)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{rate per room}} \\\\  & =252+21x-12x-{x}^{2} \\\\  & =252+9x-{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, for having $(21-x)$ rooms rented out, the hotel has a cost (in dollars) of \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(x) & =3(21-x) \\\\  & =63-3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the profit function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\ P(x) & =(252+9x-{x}^{2})-(63-3x) \\\\  & =189+12x-{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince there are $21$ rooms, the permitted domain is $0 \\leq x\\leq 21.$ \n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $0 \\lt x\\lt 21.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\nP'(x) = 12 - 2x \\,.\n$$\n\nand solving $P'(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 12-2x & =0 \\\\ 2x & =12 \\\\ x & =6\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $x=6$ lies in the interval $0 <  x < 21.$ All that's left now is to confirm that $x=6$ does indeed give a maximum value of $P(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\nP''(x) = -2 <             0\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, the point $x=6$ is indeed a local maximum of $P(x).$ And since $P(x)$ is quadratic, we conclude that $x=6$ is a global maximum of $P(x).$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that the hotel will have a maximum profit when there are $x=6$ vacant rooms.  \n\nWe know that the rate per room is $(12+x)$ dollars. If there are $6$ vacant rooms, then the rate per room is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12+6 & =\\text{\\[}math]18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the manager should charge a room rate of $\\[/math]18$ per night." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100788 #edb/ref 1069 "e-7697" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100788 #edb/ref 1071 "A textile center sells $x$ hundreds of meters of fabric at a price $p(x),$ in dollars, given by\n\n$$\np(x) = x^2 - 15x + 48 + \\dfrac{78}{x}.\n$$\n\nIf the textile center sells between $100$ and $900$ meters of fabric daily, how many meters of fabric must the textile center sell to maximize its revenue?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100788 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7996afa5-da25-51e5-915c-5f0acdccb8bd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100788 #edb/ref 1074 "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that for selling $x$ hundreds of meters in total, the total revenue $R(x)$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & =x\\cdot p(x) \\\\  & =x({x}^{2}-15x+48+\\frac{78}{x}) \\\\  & ={x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}+48x+78.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we note that the permitted domain is $1 \\leq x \\leq 9,$ since $x = 1$ corresponds to $1 \\cdot 100 = 100$ and $x = 9$ corresponds to $9 \\cdot 100 = 900.$ Therefore, $x=1$ and $x=9$ are both candidates for the global maxima of $R(x).$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $R(x)$ for $1 <         x <         9.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\nR'(x) = 3x^2 - 30x + 48,\n$$\n\nand solving $R'(x)=0$ gives the following critical points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-30x+48 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-10x+16 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)(x-8) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=2$ and $x=8.$\n\nOur candidates are $x=1,2, 8$ and $9.$ Computing $R(x)$ at each of these values, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(1) & =(1)^{3}-15(1)^{2}+48(1)+78=112 \\\\ R(2) & =(2)^{3}-15(2)^{2}+48(2)+78=122\\,✓ \\\\ R(8) & =(8)^{3}-15(8)^{2}+48(8)+78=14 \\\\ R(9) & =(9)^{3}-15(9)^{2}+48(9)+78=24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to maximize its revenue, the textile center should sell $2 \\cdot 100 = 200$ meters of fabric." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101017 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048116 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101017 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048162 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101017 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048883 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101017 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101195 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101017 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101481 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101017 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186511478 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101017 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186511484 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101017 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186511501 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101017 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186511548 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101017 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186511554 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101017 #edb/ref 1039 "Optimizing a Profit, Revenue, or Cost Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101017 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186100661 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101017 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "1aca5854-177d-5ebe-9a63-caaa7d916c4d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101018 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049326 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101018 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101562 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101018 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101563 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101018 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186511464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101018 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186511467 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101018 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186511480 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101018 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186511517 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101018 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186511536 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101018 #edb/ref 1039 "Maximizing a Revenue Function Given a Price Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101018 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186100788 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101018 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "f2d19236-d6ea-57da-b2a4-b926fdddb30e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101019 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101111 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101019 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101112 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101019 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101503 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101019 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101504 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101019 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101564 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101019 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186511520 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101019 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186511538 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101019 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186511566 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101019 #edb/ref 1039 "Maximizing a Profit Function Given a Revenue and Cost Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101019 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186100660 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101019 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "9c30919c-c2b9-5e09-8512-98fcbe339564" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101020 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101113 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101020 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101196 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101020 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101285 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101020 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101378 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101020 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101482 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101020 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186511471 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101020 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186511475 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101020 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186511522 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101020 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186511575 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101020 #edb/ref 1039 "Maximizing a Profit Function by Constructing a Revenue and Cost Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101020 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186100784 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101020 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "6d2b1036-0ea4-5fd1-bfed-885dcbae618b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101072 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186101902 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101072 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186101903 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101072 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186101904 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101072 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186101905 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101072 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186101906 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101072 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186101907 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101072 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101072 #edb/ref 1045 "Optimization Problems in Economics" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101072 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186101902 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101072 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048985 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101072 #edb/ref 1050 1500.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101072 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "0e211b6d-7f83-5173-9b92-9e29afdbbe93" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101113 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186098013 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101113 #edb/ref 1069 "q-119091" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101113 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101113 #edb/ref 1071 "A summer camp has capacity for $200$ kids. The price for each kid attending the camp is $$300.$ The cost of setting up the camp is made up of a fixed cost of $$2\\,000$ plus a cost of $$100$ per kid. How many kids should attend the summer camp to maximize its profit?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101113 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "284fac66-d855-554d-969c-bb28dda1de3d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101113 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101113 #edb/ref 1074 "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that for $x$ kids attending, the summer camp has a revenue (in dollars) of \n\n$$\nR(x)=300\\cdot x=300x\n$$\n\nand a cost (in dollars) of \n\n$$\nC(x)={{2\\,000}_{⏟}}_{\\text{fixed cost}}\\,\\,+{{100x.}_{⏟}}_{\\text{variable cost}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the profit function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\  & =300x-(2\\,000+100x) \\\\  & =200x-2\\,000.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe note that the permitted domain is $0\\le x\\le 200.$ Therefore, $x=0$ and $x=200$ are candidates for the global maxima of $P(x).$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $0<x<200.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=200.\n$$\n\nWe can see that the equation ${P}^{′}(x)=0$ has no solutions.\n\nThus, our candidates are $x=0$ and $x=200.$ Computing $P(x)$ at each of these values, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0) & =200(0)-2\\,000=-2\\,000 \\\\ P(200) & =200(200)-2\\,000=38\\,000\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the summer camp will have a maximum profit level when $200$ kids attend." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101504 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186100185 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101504 #edb/ref 1069 "q-65867" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101504 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101504 #edb/ref 1071 "A factory spends $C(x)={x}^{4}+13x$ thousand dollars to manufacture $x$ units of a certain product. The factory sells the product for $$121\\,000$ per unit. What is the maximum monthly profit that the factory can make given that it can produce at most $4$ items per month?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101504 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f5c03e3f-902a-5f23-a083-877f778bea41" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101504 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101504 #edb/ref 1074 "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that for selling $x$ units of the product per month, the factory has a monthly revenue (income) of $R(x)=121\\cdot x=121x$ thousand dollars. \n\nTherefore, the profit function (in thousands of dollars) is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\  & =121x-({x}^{4}+13x) \\\\  & =108x-{x}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe note that the permitted domain is $0\\le x\\le 4.$ Therefore, $x=0$ and $x=4$ are both candidates for the global maxima of $P(x).$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $0<x<4.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=108-4{x}^{3},\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 108-4{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =27 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll that's left now is to confirm that $x=3$ does indeed give a maximum value of $P(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(x)=-12{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating ${P}^{″}(x)$ at the critical point, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{″}(3)=-12(3)^{2}=-108<0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that $x=3$ is a maximum of $P(x).$ \n\nOur candidates are $x=0,$ $x=3$ and $x=4.$ Computing $P(x)$ at each of these values, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0) & =108(0)-{0}^{4}=0 \\\\ P(3) & =108(3)-{3}^{4}=243\\,✓ \\\\ P(4) & =108(4)-{4}^{4}=176\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum monthly profit is $$243\\,000.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101564 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186100545 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101564 #edb/ref 1069 "q-87573" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101564 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101564 #edb/ref 1071 "A computer science center organizes a machine learning course. The course is activated if there are at least $3$ participants, and it can be attended by at most $13$ participants. The price for each participant is $$200.$ The cost of providing the course is $C(x)=200x-4{x}^{3}+0.3{x}^{4},$ where $x$ is the number of participants. What is the maximum profit that the computer science center can make?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101564 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e5396c61-6040-5997-8775-94633d636485" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101564 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101564 #edb/ref 1074 "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates' test.\n\nFirst, note that for $x$ participants, the computer science center has a revenue (income) of $R(x)=200\\cdot x=200x$ dollars. \n\nTherefore, the profit function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\  & =200x-(200x-4{x}^{3}+0.3{x}^{4}) \\\\  & =4{x}^{3}-0.3{x}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe note that the permitted domain is $3\\le x\\le 13.$ Therefore, $x=3$ and $x=13$ are candidates for the global maxima of $P(x).$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $3\\ge x\\ge 13.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=12{x}^{2}-1.2{x}^{3},\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 12{x}^{2}-1.2{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ 1.2{x}^{2}(10-x) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=0$ and $x=10.$ However, the values $x=0$ is not in the permitted domain, so the only possible new candidate is $x=10.$\n\nLet's check if $x=10$ gives a maximum value of $P(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{″}(x) & =24x-3.6{x}^{2} \\\\ {P}^{″}(10) & =240-360=-120<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative at  $x=10$ is negative, the point $x=10$ is a maximum. \n\nOur candidates are $x=3,x=10$ and $x=13.$ Computing $P(x)$ at each of these values, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3) & =4(3)^{3}-0.3(3)^{4}=83.7 \\\\ P(10) & =4(10)^{3}-0.3(10)^{4}=1\\,000\\,✓ \\\\ P(13) & =4(13)^{3}-0.3(13)^{4}=219.7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the maximum profit is $$1\\,000.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101902 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101903 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101902 #edb/ref 1061 644177 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101902 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1fc21a4d-0b82-5f3e-b8d9-126d7179656f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101902 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186102014 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101903 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101904 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101903 #edb/ref 1061 644178 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101903 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "822eb919-1779-5cf4-9c9e-2c66f15e9e2a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101903 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186101017 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101904 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101905 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101904 #edb/ref 1061 644181 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101904 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9f61e403-b029-5b57-97a2-0449bc4a4ffd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101904 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186101994 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101905 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101906 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101905 #edb/ref 1061 644182 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101905 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ab8f2b97-af90-5292-ad79-44e12e42010b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101905 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186101018 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101906 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101907 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101906 #edb/ref 1061 644186 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101906 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f5614397-09dd-526a-abea-3acccf854d69" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101906 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186101019 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101907 #edb/ref 1061 644190 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101907 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ca0f41cd-a2e3-5d80-9d45-4b2c58377bc5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101907 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186101020 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101994 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that the price function $p(x)$ gives the *price per unit* at which a company sells $x$ units of a good. \n\nThe revenue function $R(x)$ associated with a price function $p(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & =(\\text{number of units})\\cdot (\\text{price per unit}) \\\\  & =x\\cdot p(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, recall that the cost function $C(x)$ associated with producing $x$ units of a good can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(x) & =(\\text{fixed cost})+(\\text{number of units})\\cdot (\\text{cost per unit}) \\\\  & =(\\text{fixed cost})+x\\cdot (\\text{cost per unit})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe quantity $x\\cdot (\\textrm{cost per unit})$ is sometimes called the **variable cost** since it is the part of the cost function $C(x)$ that is dependent on the number of units $x$ produced by the company. \n\nFinally, if we know the revenue function $R(x)$ and the cost function $C(x),$ then the profit function $P(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nP(x) = R(x) - C(x).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101994 #edb/ref 1065 4663 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101994 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "fb6a794d-d60c-5d16-8912-b69af938c892" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101994 #edb/ref 1067 "A Review of Price and Cost Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186102014 #edb/ref 1064 "There are several quantities that businesses need to keep track of to ensure that the business remains healthy:\n\n- Revenue: This is the total amount of money (in dollars) that a company gains after selling $x$ units of product.\n\n- Cost: This is the total amount of money (in dollars) that a company spends to create $x$ units of product.\n\n- Profit. The difference between revenue and cost. It represents the amount of *additional* money that the business has earned after paying its costs.\n\nBusinesses often strive to maximize revenue and profit. They also often seek to minimize cost. We can solve these problems using the second derivative or the candidates test. Let's find out how." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186102014 #edb/ref 1065 682 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186102014 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "7d29b308-bacd-5426-b6c8-43b0da8fcd75" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186102014 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510773 #edb/ref 1080 "13" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510773 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510773 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0052cd3e-3060-5730-98da-8af2605187af" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510796 #edb/ref 1080 "820" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510796 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 1759